From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wzy312 <1152543547wzy@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 14:07:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] storage: add an OHOS RDB backend for webstorage,
 client_storage and indexeddb
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add an OpenHarmony RDB backend behind the `ohos-rdb-backend` feature: OHOS
builds can use the system database through ohos-rdb-sys C FFI instead of the
bundled sqlite3. The sqlite modules keep their upstream logic; the RDB
implementation lives in new files with its own copy of the schema and CRUD, a
deliberate duplication that keeps rebases trivial, wired in through a small set
of cfg hooks. The FFI wrappers free their native handles on drop. A cursor
borrows its transaction, so the compiler rejects reads after commit. Strings
with interior NUL bytes are stored as blobs and read back unchanged.

Backend selection is a feature choice rather than a target choice, so a plain
`./mach build --ohos` keeps the upstream SQLite behaviour and no configuration
fails to compile:

  ./mach build --ohos                        # SQLite, as before
  ./mach build --ohos --features ohos-rdb-backend
                                             # both, chosen at runtime
  ./mach build --ohos --no-default-features \
    --features "base,ohos-rdb-backend,servo-allocator/use-jemalloc"
                                             # RDB only

Dropping sqlite is what makes the third configuration worth having: the
stripped aarch64 release `libservoshell.so` goes from 127,736,736 to
125,736,080 bytes, 1.91 MiB less.

With both backends compiled in, `storage_ohos_rdb_backend_enabled` (default
true) picks one per store as it is opened, so a single binary can A/B them
without reinstalling. The two backends keep disjoint on-disk stores — sqlite
files versus RDB's nested `rdb/<store>` — so flipping the pref switches to the
other backend's data, which starts out empty. That is intended for A/B
comparison; there is no migration between them.

Deliberate difference from the SQLite twin: RDB delivers the callbacks of a
batch after its commit, so the whole batch lands or errors together (SQLite
reports each operation in autocommit). Schema operations join that batch
transaction and follow the same rule. A second deliberate difference: after a
failed webstorage load on OHOS the session's final save is skipped, so a
partially loaded state cannot overwrite the store.

On a HarmonyOS phone the two legs of the pref agree exactly. One signed HAP,
`storage_ohos_rdb_backend_enabled` flipped between runs, over the ten WPT
storage tests the backend touches: 1188 subtests pass and 37 fail on each
side, test for test, and the failures are the ones tests/wpt/meta already
expects. The stores confirm the switch rather than assuming it — with the pref
on the app writes only `rdb/reg.rdb` and `rdb/webstorage.rdb`, with it off only
`reg.sqlite` and `webstorage.sqlite`.

One RDB limitation is worth recording. Opening a second client_storage registry
fails (14800001 / 14800011) about two runs in three, on DAYU200 and PLR-AL00
alike, when the process is not an application: `new_storage_threads` with no
`config_dir` falls back to `tempfile::tempdir()`, which outside an application
sandbox is `/data/local/tmp`. Neither half alone is enough — a single registry
under `/data/local/tmp` is reliable, and so are two registries inside the
application, which a phone confirmed over ten launches of the default
configuration and five of `--temporary-storage`, the latter being the same
two-registry shape that fails in a bare process. Servo always passes the
application's cache dir, so it never meets the condition;
`test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups`, which does, is ignored
when the RDB backend is compiled in.

Validation: Parallel twin of the sqlite backend — revalidate on every rebase even when it merges cleanly, since upstream storage changes never conflict with the RDB engine files. (1) `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets --features ohos-rdb-backend` must pass warning-free, as must the plain and the `--no-default-features --features ohos-rdb-backend` configurations; a new `AsyncOperation`/engine-trait variant fails here loudly and must be implemented in the RDB twins, not wildcarded. (2) Diff upstream changes since the previous base with `git log -p <old-base>..<new-base> -- components/storage` and mirror any sqlite-engine semantics changes into `engines/ohos_rdb.rs` / `webstorage/engines/ohos_rdb.rs` / `client_storage_ohos_rdb.rs`. (3) Host `./mach test-unit -p servo-storage` pins the sqlite reference behaviour. (4) On a device, run the same suite against both backends with `CARGO_TARGET_AARCH64_UNKNOWN_LINUX_OHOS_RUNNER=ohos-test-runner cargo nextest run -p servo-storage --target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos [--no-default-features --features ohos-rdb-backend]` (raise nextest's slow-timeout: every test is pushed to the device), then the same-HAP A/B: build with `--flavor=harmonyos --features ohos-rdb-backend`, launch with `--psn=--webdriver=7000 --psn=--pref=dom_indexeddb_enabled=true --psn=--pref=storage_ohos_rdb_backend_enabled=<bool>`, and drive the WPT IndexedDB + webstorage subset through `python/wpt/ohos_webdriver_test.py`'s controller — both legs must match test for test, and `bm clean -n org.servo.servo -d` between them so the on-disk store proves which backend ran.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implementation and on-device testing)
